# =============================================================================
# matrix — Matrix matmul partials are exact; a finished cell that does not fit
# the element type traps at the store.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Exact partials — the inner product of `[100, 100]` with `[1, -1]` is
#     100 − 100 = 0, computed exactly whatever the element width is.
#   • Trap at the cell store — the same row against `[1, 1]` is 200, one past
#     the largest `i8`; the finished cell does not fit its slot, so the matmul
#     aborts with "200 does not fit in `i8`" and never wraps to -56.
#   • The trap follows the destination — a `matrix<i8, …>` destination applies
#     the `i8` policy at the store, not per intermediate product.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting the product to wrap because the cells are narrow.
#   • Expecting the trap where a partial is computed — an expression is a
#     number; the policy applies where the value lands in the cell.
#
# See also: matrix, numerus
# =============================================================================

# store-overflow — exact matmul partials, checked cell store
#
# POLICY:
#   A matrix cell store applies the element type's policy. The inner product
#   is exact, so a finished cell outside the element range aborts the run
#   instead of wrapping. The first product below fits and prints; the second
#   traps before its `print`.

main {
    let matrix<i8, [1, 2]> a ← [[100, 100]] ↦ matrix<i8, [1, 2]>
    let matrix<i8, [2, 1]> b ← [[1], [-1]] ↦ matrix<i8, [2, 1]>
    let matrix<i8, [1, 1]> ok ← a · b
    print ok
    let matrix<i8, [2, 1]> c ← [[1], [1]] ↦ matrix<i8, [2, 1]>
    let matrix<i8, [1, 1]> d ← a · c
    print d
}
